    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Warnerville was originally called Mann's Valley after Captain George Mann, a Revolutionary War militiaman who settled there in 1786.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/warnerville_new_york",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Warnerville lies on New York State Route 7, about 1.4 miles from Cobleskill and 3.4 miles from Richmondville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/warnerville_new_york",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Warnerville is the site of the 1778 Battle of Cobleskill, a raid led by Mohawk leader Joseph Brant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/warnerville_new_york",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first European settlers were German immigrants George Warner and John Zea, who built the first log house in 1764.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/warnerville_new_york",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Warnerville sits within the Schoharie Creek watershed, with elevations ranging from about 500 to 1,500 feet and an average of 925 feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/warnerville_new_york",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The hamlet’s elevation of roughly 925 feet places it amid rolling hills that contrast with the higher Catskill Mountains to the south.",
